+## Command forms
+
+Targets and queries are positional: there is no `--url` or `--query` option, and `message list` takes the channel as its target rather than `--channel`. `--workspace` goes after the command, not before it.
+
+```bash
+agent-slack message get  <url>  |  <#channel|channel-id> --ts TS  [--max-body-chars N]
+agent-slack message list <url|#channel|channel-id> [--thread-ts TS] [--limit N] [--max-body-chars N]
+agent-slack search messages <query> [--channel C] [--after YYYY-MM-DD] [--max-content-chars N]
+agent-slack user get <U...|@handle>
+agent-slack channel list
+```
+
+`--max-body-chars` limits `message` output and `--max-content-chars` limits `search` output; neither command accepts the other's flag. There is no `channel info`, `user lookup`, `read`, or `thread` command.
+
 For scheduled writes, prefer `--schedule` with an ISO 8601 timestamp and explicit offset when timezone matters. Named `--schedule-in` phrases use the executing environment's local timezone; confirm that it matches the user's intent.
 
 Named `later remind --in` values such as `tomorrow` or `monday` also use the executing environment's local timezone at 9:00. Confirm that timezone or pass an explicit Unix timestamp.
